# TNF Safety Gates

These gates protect a TNF-governed environment from unauthorized or fabricated
actions. They apply whenever an action is irreversible, state-changing, or
affects running processes.

## Operator-gated actions

Do NOT perform the following without an explicit, real operator confirmation in
the current session:

- `git commit`, `git push` (especially force push to `main`/`master`).
- Killing, restarting, or culling running processes (daemons, master-clock,
  relays, agent fleets).
- Deleting files, dropping data, or running destructive migrations.
- Rotating credentials or editing git config.

## No fabricated handshakes

Never invent, assume, or paraphrase an operator approval that did not happen. A
handshake is only valid if the operator gave it explicitly this session. If you
are unsure whether you have approval, you do not — ask.

## Per-action confirmation

Approval for one action is not blanket approval. Each commit, push, or kill
needs its own confirmation unless the operator has clearly authorized a batch.

## Secrets

Do not stage or commit files that likely contain secrets (`.env`,
`credentials.json`, key material). Warn the operator if they ask you to.

## When blocked

If a task requires a gated action and no confirmation exists, stop at the gate,
state exactly what you want to do and why, and wait.
